{
  "language": "javascript",
  "case": "unread_surfaces",
  "why": "The fourth review pass. Five export surfaces this pass does not read in full \u2014 `Object.freeze({\u2026})`, `module.exports = make()`, an alias assigned after its declaration, a subscript `exports['find']`, and `Object.assign` onto an alias \u2014 each of which the third pass still treated as readable and so enforced, dropping a true call. \"Readable\" is an allowlist now, and every one of these calls is resolved by name and kept because its target exists. Plus a renamed export, `{ Handler: Impl }`, with no class called `Handler` anywhere to hide behind, so the method call must be routed through the export map before the existence check rather than after it. Plus two bindings that shadow the module's `store` over their own scope only: a `var` inside a callback (hoisted to the callback, not to `go`) and a `catch (store)` clause. A scope that runs past either loses the true `store.find()` on the last line. The fifth review pass adds five more. Two write the exports object through a channel pass 4 did not recognise, and so lost a true call: a brace-less `if (c) exports.find = find` and `Reflect.defineProperty(exports, 'find', \u2026)`. Two hand the exports object to something the reader cannot follow, an alias `const api = exports` and an argument `mixin(exports, \u2026)`, so their calls go by name. And two refusals: `frozen.secret()` and `shadow.secret()` name functions their module declares but never exports, which a name lookup would reach. The fifth review pass (N5): the `{ Handler: Impl }` refusal could not fire \u2014 its target names no node \u2014 so `decoyed.js` repeats the shape with a decoy `class Handler`, called from JavaScript, where a reader that ignores the rename lands on the decoy. A file that declares its own `var module` exports nothing through it. A `let module` inside a top-level block binds the block, not the file; an own-module file's `module.exports.X` is a local's too.",
